---
// The taxonomy (2026-09-29), on the model of arXiv's category taxonomy: the registry's scope, then
// the categories it classifies papers by, the harvester's own vocabulary (oscr/vocabulary/
// categories.json, oscr/classify.py) with each value's definition and its count in the build's
// export, then OpenAlex's topics and the papers' types, as counted. One page.
import Info from "../components/Info.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../config";
import { catalog, number, withCode, withPage } from "../lib/catalog";
import { categories } from "../lib/entities";
import { STATUSES } from "../lib/status";
import { pageOf, typeLabel } from "../lib/paper";
import { SCOPE, TAXONOMY, VOCABULARY } from "../lib/taxonomy";

const f = catalog.figures;
const statuses = (f.statuses ?? {}) as unknown as Record<string, number>;
const byType = Object.entries((f.by_type ?? {}) as unknown as Record<string, number>).sort((x, y) => y[1] - x[1]);
const yes = SCOPE.values.find((v) => v.value === "yes");
const no = SCOPE.values.find((v) => v.value === "no");
// OpenAlex's primary topic of each paper with a page: its domain, then its field.
const domains = new Map<string, { papers: number; fields: Map<string, number> }>();
let topics = 0;
for (const a of withPage) {
  const t = pageOf(a)?.overview?.topic;
  if (!t?.domain) continue;
  topics += 1;
  const d = domains.get(t.domain) ?? { papers: 0, fields: new Map() };
  d.papers += 1;
  d.fields.set(t.field, (d.fields.get(t.field) ?? 0) + 1);
  domains.set(t.domain, d);
}
const domainList = [...domains].sort((x, y) => y[1].papers - x[1].papers);
const threshold = Math.round(categories.min_confidence * 100);
const PAGE = ["code_verified", "code_found", "code_empty", "code_dead", "on_request", "data_only"];
const toc = [
  { id: "scope", text: "Scope" },
  { id: "pages", text: "Which papers have a page" },
  { id: "guide", text: "How to read the categories" },
  ...TAXONOMY.map((t) => ({ id: t.facet, text: t.name })),
  { id: "method", text: "How a paper gets its categories" },
  { id: "openalex", text: "OpenAlex's topics" },
  { id: "types", text: "Types of papers" },
];
---
<Info
  title="Taxonomy"
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} covers and how it classifies papers: its scope, its categories (modality, organism, population, subfield) with their definitions and counts, OpenAlex's topics and the papers' types.`}
  group="about"
  path="/taxonomy/"
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead" slot="lead">
    {SITE_NAME} covers neuroscience, and classifies each paper it reads along four facets: the kind of data it collects
    or analyses, the species studied, the condition studied, and its angle. The definitions below are the ones its
    classifier and its annotators use; the counts are those of the catalogue this site was built from.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scope">Scope</h2>
  <p>The registry's field is neuroscience, defined so: {SCOPE.definition}</p>
  <dl class="terms">
    {yes && (<><dt>In scope</dt><dd>{yes.definition} For example: {(yes.examples ?? []).join("; ")}.</dd></>)}
    {no && (<><dt>Out of scope</dt><dd>{no.definition} For example: {(no.examples ?? []).join("; ")}.</dd></>)}
  </dl>
  <p>
    <strong>How papers come in.</strong> The harvester asks Europe PMC for the open-access papers whose full text it holds
    and whose title or abstract names a neuroscience term, EEG, MEG, fMRI, intracranial recordings, spike sorting,
    calcium imaging, electrophysiology, brain–computer interfaces, neuroimaging, fNIRS, transcranial magnetic or deep
    brain stimulation, neural oscillations, the connectome, diffusion MRI, neurons, neural, cortex, cortical,
    hippocampus, brain. The query is broad on purpose, and catches papers that are not neuroscience ("neural network",
    "cortical bone"): the classification then decides.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why off-topic papers stay out.</strong> A paper judged out of scope stays on the operator's computer: it has
    no page, is in no list, search, lookup or dataset, and counts in no figure. Harvesting broadly and filtering after
    keeps the registry complete for its field without letting unrelated papers dilute its rates, a decision of 27
    September 2026. {number(f.articles)} papers read are in scope today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pages">Which papers have a page</h2>
  <p>Among the papers in scope, those with something to show about code or data have a page; every other one is known to the <a href="/lookup/">DOI lookup</a>.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Page</th><th class="n">Papers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(STATUSES).map(([k, s]) => (
          <tr><td>{s.tone ? <span class={s.tone}>{s.label}</span> : s.label}</td><td>{PAGE.includes(k) ? "yes" : "no: the lookup"}</td><td class="n">{number(statuses[k] ?? 0)}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>{number(withPage.length)} papers have a page, {number(withCode.length)} of them with their authors' code. <a href="/help/paper/#status">What each status means.</a></p>

  <h2 id="guide">How to read the categories</h2>
  <p>
    Each facet has a fixed list of values. A value has a key (<code>modality.eeg</code>), a name, a definition and
    examples, and the number of papers with a page it applies to (with, in brackets, those with their authors' code);
    its name leads to its page on <a href="/browse/">Browse</a> when it has papers. Modality, organism and population
    take several values per paper; the subfield takes one. The vocabulary's version: {VOCABULARY.version}.
  </p>

  {TAXONOMY.map((t) => (
    <>
      <h2 id={t.facet}>{t.name}</h2>
      <p>{t.definition} {t.multi ? "A paper may have several." : "One per paper."}</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="facts">
          <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Definition</th><th class="n">Papers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {t.values.map((v) => (
              <tr id={v.id}>
                <td><strong>{v.url && v.papers > 0 ? <a href={v.url}>{v.name}</a> : v.name}</strong><br /><code>{v.id}</code></td>
                <td>{v.definition}{(v.examples ?? []).length > 0 && <><br /><span class="muted">For example: {(v.examples ?? []).join("; ")}.</span></>}</td>
                <td class="n">{number(v.papers)} ({number(v.withCode)})</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
            {t.others.map((v) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{v.url ? <a href={v.url}>{v.name}</a> : v.name}</strong></td>
                <td class="muted">A value outside the current vocabulary, from an earlier label.</td>
                <td class="n">{number(v.papers)} ({number(v.withCode)})</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 id="method">How a paper gets its categories</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Rules first.</strong> Weighted patterns run over the paper's title (strongest), keywords, MeSH terms,
      subjects, abstract and journal; its type and methods add weak hints. Every value comes with its confidence and the
      reasons that fired ("title: 'EEG'"). When the rules find nothing, or two values compete closely, the facet is
      marked ambiguous rather than guessed.</li>
    <li><strong>The operator's labels win.</strong> Where the operator has labelled a paper by hand, the label overrides
      the rules; a sample of such labels serves to measure them.</li>
    <li><strong>A local model for the ambiguous cases</strong>: once one has been compared with the operator's labels
      and chosen. It runs on the operator's computer, and uses its graphics processor only between 01:00 and 07:00. None
      is in use yet.</li>
    <li><strong>What is shown.</strong> A category appears on the site when the operator or a model set it, or the rules
      did with a confidence of at least {threshold}% and no ambiguity. Otherwise the paper is simply not in that
      category: an unclassified paper is still in <a href="/list/">the list</a> and the search.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="openalex">OpenAlex's topics</h2>
  <p>
    OpenAlex, an open index of research, gives each paper a primary topic within its own hierarchy, domain, field,
    subfield, topic. {SITE_NAME} shows it on each paper's page as OpenAlex gives it; it is not the registry's
    classification. {topics > 0
      ? `Among the papers with a page, ${number(topics)} have one:`
      : "No paper of this catalogue has one yet."}
  </p>
  {domainList.length > 0 && (
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="facts">
        <thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Fields, with their papers</th><th class="n">Papers</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {domainList.map(([domain, d]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{domain}</td>
              <td>{[...d.fields].sort((x, y) => y[1] - x[1]).map(([field, n]) => `${field} (${number(n)})`).join(", ")}</td>
              <td class="n">{number(d.papers)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  )}

  <h2 id="types">Types of papers</h2>
  <p>
    The papers read in scope, by their type as the publisher declares it. The registry's rates, the share of papers
    with their authors' code, are computed on research articles only (research articles, brief reports, methods
    articles, data papers, rapid communications, short reports): reviews, conference abstracts, case reports and notices
    rarely have code of their own.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th class="n">Papers read</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{byType.map(([t, n]) => <tr><td>{typeLabel(t) || t}</td><td class="n">{number(n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Info>
